comments.body:
@participant2 Thanks; Indeed it is more of a hypothesis iff a non-existent key overwrites the default-to-empty string in PHP, i.e., triggers an error. However, in a quick test it does not:
```
<?php
        $a = array();
        print($a['c']['k'] ?? "foo");
?>
```
Still, I am not a programmer/dev, so i might err there.

Also, from a user perspective, it does not really matter if "the default changed" or "the default did not change, but the behavior changed". Anyway, guess it is best to wait for the devs assessment there.

Also thanks for pointing out the point about the global address books. This was luckily already set to off, as I tend to be rather conscious about these things. Please note, though, that if similar behavior exists there, this may be an even more significant PII leak than the user database, as it will include information on non-local people and their connections to local users.

Similarly, if the statement on using HTTP is true, this is another severe issue, as it means that the system was not sufficiently suited to handle PII (even iff users' explicit consent was in play).

Also, taking a look at the identityproof mechanic might be a good idea, in case this also allows user enumeration.

@participant4 you might have to scroll down in the settings page. It is not exactly clear; Even I was duped a few minutes ago despite actually knowing where it is while checking up on the outbound lookup-setting.

@reporter yes, that is already being worked on, see https://github.com/nextcloud/server/pull/51352 but I prioritized a quick release with a hard-coded turned-off over this one, so this would than ship in a later release, best case with the April maintenance release. I didn't want to delay the current release and its hard-coded quick fix for the sake of a proper solution for the price of a longer wait for people for a release.

So it is band-aid fix turning it off in-code for march and shipping an off by default afterwards while I still need folks to look into the deletion issue you reported because I don't need to bring back the feature even with off-by-default if we have a bug in the deletion logic.
So this needs to be clarified before the whole construct with the lookup server would come back to life at all.
